{
  "prompt": "Explain the three categories of VR systems to a classmate who missed the lecture.",
  "reference_answer": "VR systems are categorized based on the degree of immersion, which refers to how much the user's senses are blocked from the real world. The first category, non-immersive, involves virtual experiences through computer displays, such as gaming, where the user controls characters but the environment is not directly interacting with them. The second, semi-immersive, offers a partial experience, often using a VR headset, where some senses are free to sense the real world. Finally, fully immersive systems completely block all the user's senses, requiring extensive devices like body suits and helmets to create a sense of total presence in the virtual world.",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "Categories are based on degree of immersion.",
      "keywords": ["degree of immersion", "senses are blocked"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Non-immersive systems use computer displays and allow physical environment control.",
      "keywords": ["computer displays", "physical environment"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Semi-immersive systems provide partial experience, often using a VR headset.",
      "keywords": ["partial experience", "VR headset"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fully immersive systems completely block all senses for total presence.",
      "keywords": ["completely blocking", "sense of total presence"]
    }
  ]
}

