{
  "prompt": "How do we adapt the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) when the initial graph does not have direct connections between all nodes?",
  "reference_answer": "The initial problem requires finding the minimum travel time path that visits all nodes, but the graph may not be complete. To solve this, we introduce hypothetical edges between vertices that lack a direct connection. The weight of these new hypothetical edges is defined as the shortest path length between those two vertices. This shortest path length is calculated by summing the edge weights of the shortest path in the original connected graph. By doing this, we create a modified graph that becomes a solvable instance of the TSP problem.",
