{
  "prompt": "Compare and contrast the Vector Scan device and the Raster Scan device. How do their rendering methods differ, and in what types of settings is each typically applied?",
  "reference_answer": "The two devices differ fundamentally in how they view the image. A vector scan device treats the image as continuous geometric primitives, rendering by exciting only selective pixels that define those primitives. Conversely, a raster scan device views the image as represented by the whole pixel grid, considering all pixels for rendering in a sequence, typically from left to right, top to bottom. Because vector scan devices require high precision to target specific pixels, they are costly and are typically used in specialized settings. Raster scan devices, by considering all pixels, do not require this high precision.",
