  "reference_answer": "Hierarchical processing is a neural mechanism where signals propagate from sense organ receptors through the neural network to the cerebral cortex. Initially, each receptor responds to a narrow range of stimuli across time, space, and frequency. As these signals pass through several neurons, inputs from numerous different receptors are simultaneously taken into account. This process works in stages, with each level of aggregation combining lower-level information to detect increasingly complex patterns in the stimulus. Ultimately, this allows the brain to detect these complex patterns as the processing reaches the cerebral cortex.",
